{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-13775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}4{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028924/state.json" "file_sha256" "c22cf7f0dbc285a1a217a2c0ab768006228ca003815a9e100d048618533b3744"} {"question" "q-13775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we let $u=6x$ then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration,  we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}4{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{u}\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{6})}{e}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({e}^{u}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{6})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({2}^{6}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(64-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 63 \\\\  & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028924/state.json" "file_sha256" "c22cf7f0dbc285a1a217a2c0ab768006228ca003815a9e100d048618533b3744"} {"question" "q-13775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028924/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b2eb951cb3d36b14d96017da3418997a02a66b8f7e27d6df22722526b30e7a32"} {"question" "q-13775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.ty